<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Serbian Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1346 CE – 1371 CE · capital Skopje</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Stefan Dušan's short-lived Serbian Empire, the largest medieval Balkan state of its day, with its own patriarchate and law code.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a> from 1331, took advantage of the Byzantine civil war to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. In 1346 he raised the Serbian archbishopric to a patriarchate at Peć and was crowned Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dušan ruled through Serbian nobles and Byzantine-style titles, issuing a law code, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusans-code">Dušan's Code</a>, in 1349 and 1354. Greek lands kept Byzantine administration, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a> financed the state. Monasteries on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/monastic-community-of-mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> received his patronage. He aimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> itself but died suddenly in 1355.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Uroš V</a> the great nobles became effectively independent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> destroyed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">Vukašin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-uglesa">Jovan Uglješa</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> in 1371, and Uroš died the same year, ending the empire. Its lands passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moravian-serbia">Moravian Serbia</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a> and other successors.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/serbian-empire.jpg" alt="Serbian Empire" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Serbian_Empire,_reconstruction.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1346 CE – 1371 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Skopje</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stefan Dušan, Uroš V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Serbian; Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>, <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tsardom of Serbia, Dušan's Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1358&amp;focus=serbian-empire" aria-label="Serbian Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5201 683 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Serbian Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5521 939L5521 939M5521 939L5513 889M5521 939L5512 917M5521 939L5526 959M5521 939L5576 968M5521 939L5478 988M5521 939L5476 880M5521 939L5455 925M5521 939L5596 994M5521 939L5519 1030"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5521 939h0M5513 889h0M5512 917h0M5526 959h0M5576 968h0M5478 988h0M5476 880h0M5455 925h0M5596 994h0M5519 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 